review_text:
I picked up A Long Way Gone by Ishmael Beah after I saw another student in one of my classes reading the novel. 
 A Long Way Gone would have been a lot better if it was a more personal reconciliation or reflection. I'm not one to tell someone how to look back on their life and how to write it but the story as so choppy that I had a hard time following along. 
 And, apparently, there is a lot of controversy if Beah actually experienced all he claimed he did. 
 "I became afraid, since I could no longer tell the difference between dream and reality." {pg. 15} 
 "These days I live in three worlds: my dreams, and the experiences of my new life, which trigger memories from the past." {pg. 20} 
 The story seems to be intent on talking about the killing, the blood, the drugs, yet the story doesn't seem really tell a story that sucks you in. And the topics jump around too much, leaving the reader wondering the conclusion of a particular section.
